window.docContents[32].resolve({"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Lean___s-Trusted-Computing-Base":{"contents":"\n\nOne question that arises with any automated proof assistant\nis \"why should we trust it?\" — i.e., what if there is a bug in\nthe implementation that renders all its reasoning suspect?While it is impossible to allay such concerns completely, the fact\nthat Lean is based on the Curry-Howard correspondence gives it a\nstrong foundation. Because propositions are just types and proofs\nare just terms, checking that an alleged proof of a proposition is\nvalid just amounts to type-checking the term.  Type checkers are\nrelatively small and straightforward programs, so the \"trusted\ncomputing base\" for Lean — the part of the code that we have to\nbelieve is operating correctly — is small too.How, then, does Lean relegate its large set of complex behaviors to a small codebase?Lean is organized in two layers. The elaborator is large and\ncomplicated: it handles notation, implicit arguments, match,\nrecursion, and tactics. Its output is a plain term in Lean's core\ntype theory, which it hands to the kernel. Only the kernel is\ntrusted. It is small, and its job is just to type-check that term,\ncomputing as needed to decide whether two types are the same.This division means that a bug in the elaborator, or in a tactic,\ncannot lead Lean to accept a false theorem: at worst, it produces a\nterm that the kernel then rejects. Every declaration, whether written\nas a term or built by tactics, is checked by the kernel when it is\nadded to the environment.Two checks you might expect a typechecker to perform, exhaustiveness\nof match and termination of recursive functions, happen in the\nelaborator, because the kernel's language actually has no match and no\nrecursion at all. The kernel's language for destructuring values only has the notion of a\nrecursor: a primitive function that is exhaustive and\nterminating by construction. If the elaborator cannot express a definition\nin terms of the recursors generated by the kernel, it rejects the definition.There are some mild exceptions to this, like partial def, but those exceptions\nhave strict rules imposed upon them to prevent a proof of False,\nand in general it is the translation to recursors that catches such bugs.We can see the elaborator refuse\ndefinitions that would otherwise let us prove False:theorem or_bogus : ∀ (P Q : Prop), P ∨ Q → P :=\n  fun (P Q : Prop) (A : P ∨ Q) =>\n    match A with\n    | Or.inl h => h\nAll the types here match correctly, but the match only\nconsiders one of the possible constructors for Or.  Lean's\nexhaustiveness check will reject this definition.def infinite_loop {α : Type} (n : Nat) : α :=\n  infinite_loop n\ntermination_by structural n\n\ntheorem falso : False := infinite_loop 0\nRecursive function infinite_loop purports to return a\nvalue of any type α that you would like.  (The\ntermination_by structural n annotation tells Lean that it recurses\non argument n, not α.)  Were Lean to allow infinite_loop, then falso\nwould be definable, thus giving evidence for False.  So Lean rejects\ninfinite_loop.Besides the kernel, we also trust the handful of axioms Lean\nassumes, such as propext. The command #print axioms shows\nwhich axioms a theorem depends on. The tactic native_decide goes\nfurther, trusting Lean's compiler too, which is why careful\ndevelopments avoid it.\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009ProofObjects: The Curry-Howard Correspondence","header":"10.8. Lean's Trusted Computing Base","id":"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Lean___s-Trusted-Computing-Base"}});